course: dictionary
lesson: 3288
title: A word from old letters
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are very old, and you find them mostly in old books, old letters and law writing. Esquire is one of them. The short form is Esq., and it comes after a man's name as a polite title, like a sign of respect.
explain: Use the context. «Alice's Right Foot, Esq., Hearthrug, near the Fender» is written like an address on an envelope. Ask: what kind of word fits right after a man's name on a letter? A polite title fits, so Esq. must be one.
explain: Esquire has a second meaning from history. An esquire was a young man who served a knight, carried his shield and was on the way to becoming a knight himself. This is also called a squire. Read the sentence first, then decide which meaning fits.
fact: Esquire is a noun, and its short form is Esq.
fact: «Esq.» is a polite title written after a man's name on letters and envelopes, as in John Smith, Esq.
fact: In history, an esquire was a young man of good family who served a knight and carried his shield. This is also called a squire.
fact: In the USA, Esq. is also used after the names of lawyers.
fact: In «Alice in Wonderland», Alice grows so tall that her feet are far away, and the joke is an address for «Alice's Right Foot, Esq., Hearthrug, near the Fender».
exercise: On an envelope, what does «Esq.» after a name show? | Mud on the post | *Respect for a gentleman | A street number | A cheap present
exercise: In «Alice's Right Foot, Esq., Hearthrug, near the Fender», what is a hearthrug? | A type of shoe | A cup of tea | A small bird | *The rug in front of the fireplace
exercise: In history, what did an esquire do? | Built bridges for the river | Sold horses at the market | Ruled a castle as its king | *Served a knight and carried his shield
test: Which is the short form of esquire? | Sqr. | Esk. | Eq. | *Esq.
test: Why is Alice's foot given an address in the joke? | *Her feet are so far away that the foot seems like a person | Her foot wants to write a letter | Her foot is very small and lost | It is the name of a castle
exam: In «John Smith, Esq.», what does Esq. show? | *A polite title for a gentleman | A street name | A job as a cook | A year of birth
